{
  "result": "There is a quiet revolution that happens every time someone opens a book. In that simple act, the boundaries of a single life expand. Suddenly, one can walk the streets of a city that no longer exists, feel the weight of decisions made centuries ago, or sit beside a stranger whose joys and sorrows become, for a few hours, intimately known. Books do not demand that we agree with them; they ask only that we listen.\n\nI remember the first time a story truly transported me. I was young, curled in a chair too big for my frame, and the afternoon light fell across the pages in golden bars. Outside, the ordinary world continued\u2014cars passing, neighbors talking\u2014but inside the book, a storm was rising over an imaginary sea. I could almost taste the salt. When I looked up, the room felt smaller, as if reality had politely stepped aside to make room for something larger.\n\nThat sensation has never entirely left me. Even now, amid crowded days and endless notifications, a few paragraphs can restore a sense of spaciousness. The mind stretches. Empathy grows in directions I did not plan. Problems that seemed intractable shrink to manageable size because I have briefly inhabited other perspectives.\n\nPerhaps this is why libraries and bookstores feel like sanctuaries. They hold not just paper and ink, but countless invitations to become more than we were when we walked through the door. In a world that often urges us to hurry, reading remains a radical act of slowing down, of choosing depth over speed, and of remembering that the most meaningful journeys sometimes begin with nothing more than turning a page.",
